    "memory"
  ],
  "intro": {
    "text": "An **f-string** is a string with an `f` just before the opening quote. Each pair of curly brackets `{ }` is a **hole**: Python runs the code inside it and puts the result's text there.\n\n```\nname = \"Sam\"\nage = 12\nprint(f\"{name} is {age}\")\n```\n\nprints Sam is 12. A hole can hold any expression, like `{age + 1}`.\n\nAfter a colon inside the hole comes a **format spec**, which says how to show the value:\n\n- `{price:.2f}`: exactly 2 digits after the point, rounded\n- `{name:10}`: at least 10 characters wide. Text sits on the left, numbers on the right\n- `<`, `>` and `^` choose left, right or centre: `{name:>10}`\n- width and decimals together: `{price:>8.2f}`\n- `{big:,}` adds commas: 1,500,000\n- `{total=}` shows the code and its value, like total=42, handy for checking your work\n\n`print` has two helpers too: `sep=` sets what goes **between** the values (a space by default) and `end=` sets what goes **at the end** (a new line by default).\n\nThe **f-string** panel shows each hole get its value and then its shape. Formatting only makes text; the numbers themselves never change.",
    "analogy": "A form with empty boxes. Each {hole} is a box, the value is written into it, and the spec after the colon says how wide the box is and how many decimals to write."
  },
